  "live_records": {
    "q-110294": {
      "dom_id": "step-q110294",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that an elementary matrix is obtained by performing exactly one elementary row operation on the identity matrix $I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:=(-3){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from $I$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $B$ are elementary matrices.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
